              {
                "text": "Let me understand the rule and create 5 demonstration pairs.\n\nThe rule: given input H\u00d7W, create modified grid where for each colored (non-black) cell at (r,c), add red (2) at ((r-1) mod H, (c-1) mod W). Then tile 3\u00d73.\n\nLet me just create various inputs and let the environment compute outputs.\n\nI'll create 5 diverse small grids. Let me pick:\n\n1. 2\u00d72 with [0,0],[7,0] - but that's the example. Let me use different.\n\n2. 2\u00d73 grid\n3. 3\u00d73 grid\n4. 3\u00d72 grid\n5. 2\u00d72 grid different\n\nLet me design inputs to reveal the wrapping and tiling clearly.\n\nImportant note: verifier transform actually iterates and for cells where src_color != 0 (from (r+1,c+1)) sets out to 2 only if out[r][c]==0. This is essentially placing red at wrap-around up-left. But careful: within 3x3 tiling, the src uses modulo which is same as tile. The condition out[r][c]==0 means if a colored cell already there, don't overwrite. Fine.\n\nLet me create inputs.\n\nPair 1: 3\u00d73\n[0,0,0]\n[0,5,0]\n[0,0,0]\n\nThe 5 at (1,1) -> red at (0,0). Tiled.\n\nPair 2: 2\u00d73\n[0,3,0]\n[0,0,0]\n\n3 at (0,1) -> red at (-1 mod 2, 0) = (1,0).\n\nPair 3: 3\u00d71\n[4]\n[0]\n[0]\n\n4 at (0,0) -> red at (2,2)? wait W=1, c-1 mod 1 = 0, r-1 mod 3 = 2. So (2,0).\n\nPair 4: 4\u00d74 with multiple colors\nActually let me keep various.\n\nPair 5: 1\u00d73\n[0,9,0] -> 9 at (0,1) -> red at (0,0).\n\nLet me submit them via the tool. I can make multiple calls in one block.\n\nLet me choose:\n1. 3\u00d73 single center\n2. 2\u00d73\n3. 3\u00d71\n4. 1\u00d73\n5. 4\u00d72 with two colors\n\nLet me be careful the grids are rectangular and non-empty.\n\nI'll call show_transformed_input for each.",
